WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE
Tutorial 4 — Verifying Credentials Through the REST API.
Planning timestamps generated from the script's scene budgets.
Re-time against the final edit before publishing (see docs/video-tutorials.md).

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.400
As a verifier, you don't need a Stellar
wallet.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:14.400
You call the QuorumProof REST API with an
API key and get a signed-off answer

3
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:20.772
backed by the ledger.

4
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:52.400
Start by fetching the credential.

5
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:59.400
The response shows the issuer, the
subject, the credential type, the metadata

6
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:06.400
hash, and whether it's been revoked or has
expired.

7
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:07.400
To check many candidates, use the batch
endpoint.

8
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:14.400
Send a list of credential IDs and claim
types.

9
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:21.400
Duplicates are merged automatically.

10
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:28.400
Each result has a status: verified,
failed, not found, revoked, expired, or

11
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.200
error.

12
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:38.200
The summary at the bottom gives the
totals.

13
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:07.400
Errors follow the Problem Details
standard, with a type, title, status, and

14
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.514
detail.

15
00:04:09.614 --> 00:04:16.514
A four hundred means the request was
invalid.

16
00:04:16.614 --> 00:04:23.514
A four twenty nine means you're rate
limited; wait for the number of seconds in

17
00:04:23.614 --> 00:04:30.514
the Retry-After header, then try again.

18
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:22.400
You looked up a credential, verified a
batch, and handled errors.

19
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:29.400
Treat revoked and expired as hard failures,
and record the digest in your own

20
00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.526
audit log.

21
00:05:31.626 --> 00:05:38.526
Next, holders learn how to share a
credential with you.
